isions you took in  missions impacted the way the story  plays out  perhaps taking out a major henchman or  preventing an arms delivery in  early missions could make later missions  easier or change the course of the story  entirely maybe it's too much to ask for  from a licensed game but such a system  would suit io's ponchon for replayable  missions  and offer players the chance to really  make the story their own  maybe it's just me but for all of  diana's detailed briefings i rarely  think of hitman's targets as  anything more than a bad person who  needs killing the exception to this is  actually sean bean's mark faber aka the  undying  an elusive target who borrows much from  bean's own acting career  including from his memorable turn as  goldeneye's turncoat 006  alec trevelyan for me a good villain  goes an  awfully long way to helping a bond story  feel better than the sum of its parts  even some of my least favorite bond  films have great villains like  max zorin of a view to a kill or messes  winton kid from diamonds are forever  and if io manages to write a compelling  bad guy it's halfway to a good bond game  already  pathetic but of course there's no sense  in having a good villain  if bond can't off them in some kind of  iconic fashion  and if io can draw on their experience  with hitman  then this should be no problem please  give me bubbling radioactive pools of  water  give me shark inflating pellets give me  terrifying chainsaw torpedoes  james bond in space i mean why not eh  moonraker is definitely one of the most  outlandish bond films but i say why not  lean into the fun  and jet off into outer space it's not as  if 47 is likely to be branching out into  extraterrestrial contracts any time soon  and imagine the fun  io could have designing a mission on a  bad guy's secret space station  venting villains out into the void or  turning artificial gravity systems off  okay so it's a long shot but as the kids  say you only live twice oh for god's  